{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HRyEgVJ2yLUzgdNqNKmewc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the research context carefully to define three tiers for the Dream simulation.\n\nKey facts from the registry:\n- Static LR: crossing_rate 46.5%, lead_mean -2 (on r1-8b)\n- Temporal LR W=15: crossing_rate 76.7%, lead_mean -1.09 (on r1-8b)\n- GRU multi-layer: crossing_rate 76.7%, lead_mean +4.7, median 0, pos_lead 13/33 (39%)\n\n\n- Oracle achieves perfect detection at 100% with 76.7% early detection and mean lead of +31, while TrajGuard's Mahalanobis distance approach severely underperforms at 30.2% crossing rate. The text baseline shows moderate performance around 66-68% accuracy with 0.74-0.76 AUROC, but hidden-state representations provide a substantial 15-18 percentage point advantage, though bootstrap confidence intervals reveal considerable uncertainty in these estimates.\n\nThe remaining experiments will test cross-architecture generalization on 32B models, evaluate BOCPD for change-point detection timing, assess false positive rates on safe traces, and clarify whether the text baseline gap is fundamental or addressable. The core question is whether temporal probing's advantage holds across different model scales and whether alternative detection methods can achieve reliable positive lead times without spurious detections. persistence cross-architecture\n\nLet me define the tiers:\n\n**Best case**: \n- Cross-architecture holds (temporal advantage replicates on 32B models)\n- BOCPD achieves reliable positive lead time (better than GRU)\n- FPR on safe traces is low (<10%)\n- Hidden-state advantage over text holds cross-architecture\n- All 3 claims validated as stated\n\n**Median case**:\n- Cross-architecture partially holds (temporal advantage exists but weaker)\n- BOCPD doesn't beat GRU on lead time, or only marginal improvement\n- FPR moderate (10-25%)\n- Hidden-state advantage narrows on some models\n- Claims 1-2 hold, Claim 3 needs softening\n\n**Worst case**:\n- Cross-architecture fails (temporal advantage doesn't generalize, or text baseline matches hidden-state on 32B)\n- BOCPD fails entirely\n- FPR is high (>25%) making coverage numbers meaningless\n- Hidden-state advantage disappears on 32B (text baseline comparable)\n- Need to reframe: single-model empirical study or focus on diagnostic analysis\n\nNow I'm setting concrete thresholds for each experiment. For the cross-architecture tests, I'm looking for temporal crossing rates of 70% or higher on both 32B models in the best case, dropping to 55-70% for median performance, and below 55% signals failure. For BOCPD, I need lead times averaging at least 8 steps with a positive lead rate above 60% to validate the approach, whereas anything below 3 steps on average indicates it's not working.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11771,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11771},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3db07e5b-d1ca-4a83-964c-45ed9f8b25b8","uuid":"a25f40e9-2fd0-4034-96b4-5334ae84ac5f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HRyEgVJ2yLUzgdNqNKmewc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"Cross-architecture replicates, BOCPD achieves reliable lead time, FPR is low — all 3 claims validated as stated, paper is a clean empirical contribution with novel method component\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - Temporal LR coverage advantage (+30pp over static) replicates on both 32B models (Qwen-based)\n      - BOCPD achieves reliable positive lead time (median >0, >55% early detection rate), surpassing GRU\n      - FPR on safe traces <10% at operating threshold, confirming coverage is deployment-meaningful\n      - Hidden-state 15+pp accuracy advantage over text baseline holds cross-architecture\n      - Cross-model transfer (Llama→Qwen) achieves >60% of within-family performance\n    number_ranges:\n      cross_arch_temporal_crossing_rate: [0.70, 0.85]\n      cross_arch_static_crossing_rate: [0.30, 0.50]\n      cross_arch_temporal_lead_mean: [-2, 2]\n      bocpd_lead_mean: [8, 20]\n      bocpd_lead_median: [3, 12]\n      bocpd_positive_lead_rate: [0.55, 0.80]\n      fpr_at_operating_threshold: [0.02, 0.10]\n      hs_vs_text_acc_gap_32b: [0.12, 0.20]\n      cross_transfer_coverage_retention: [0.60, 0.80]\n    narrative_direction: \"All 3 claims hold as framed. Claim 1 (static fails) generalizes cross-architecture. Claim 2 (temporal saturates oracle) confirmed on 4 models, 2 families, 2 scales. Claim 3 upgraded: BOCPD achieves reliable pre-textual detection where GRU failed, constituting a genuine methods contribution. Story: 'temporal context is necessary AND sufficient for coverage; change-point detection unlocks timing.' Text baseline comparison cleanly demonstrates hidden-state unique value. Paper submits to EMNLP Main with strong empirical + methods narrative.\"\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"Cross-architecture partially replicates with weaker effect sizes, BOCPD marginal or inconsistent, FPR moderate — Claims 1-2 hold with caveats, Claim 3 remains 'unreliable positive', paper needs tighter scoping\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - Temporal LR advantage exists on 32B but weaker (+15-25pp vs +30pp on r1-8b) or inconsistent across models\n      - BOCPD does not clearly beat GRU on lead time (similar mean, still median ~0)\n      - FPR moderate (10-25%) requiring precision-recall tradeoff discussion\n      - Hidden-state advantage narrows to 8-15pp on some 32B models (text is competitive on coverage though not accuracy)\n      - Cross-model transfer degrades significantly (Llama→Qwen <50% retention)\n    number_ranges:\n      cross_arch_temporal_crossing_rate: [0.55, 0.72]\n      cross_arch_static_crossing_rate: [0.35, 0.55]\n      cross_arch_temporal_lead_mean: [-4, 0]\n      bocpd_lead_mean: [2, 8]\n      bocpd_lead_median: [0, 2]\n      bocpd_positive_lead_rate: [0.35, 0.55]\n      fpr_at_operating_threshold: [0.10, 0.25]\n      hs_vs_text_acc_gap_32b: [0.08, 0.15]\n      cross_transfer_coverage_retention: [0.35, 0.55]\n    narrative_direction: \"Claim 1 holds but needs qualifier ('on most architectures tested'). Claim 2 softens from 'saturates oracle' to 'substantially improves over static' — coverage advantage is real but model-dependent (+15-30pp range). Claim 3 unchanged (GRU/BOCPD both achieve positive mean but unreliable). Cross-architecture story becomes 'phenomenon is consistent but effect magnitudes vary.' Drop 'cross-model transfer' as a claim, report as ablation. FPR discussion added: precision-recall curves show usable operating points exist but require threshold tuning. Reframe contribution as 'first systematic evaluation revealing what does/doesn't transfer.' Submit EMNLP Main but prepare for Findings redirect.\"\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"Cross-architecture fails or text baseline matches hidden-state on 32B, FPR invalidates coverage metric, BOCPD adds nothing — core motivation (hidden-state unique value) collapses, must reframe entirely while targeting same venue\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - Temporal LR advantage does not replicate on 32B (static matches temporal, or both fail, or one model works and one doesn't)\n      - BOCPD performs no better than naive threshold on GRU scores\n      - FPR >25% — coverage numbers are inflated by indiscriminate firing\n      - Text baseline achieves comparable or better coverage/lead on 32B models (hidden-state advantage disappears at scale)\n      - Cross-model transfer essentially fails (<35% retention)\n    number_ranges:\n      cross_arch_temporal_crossing_rate: [0.35, 0.55]\n      cross_arch_static_crossing_rate: [0.35, 0.55]\n      cross_arch_temporal_lead_mean: [-6, -1]\n      bocpd_lead_mean: [-2, 4]\n      bocpd_lead_median: [-2, 0]\n      bocpd_positive_lead_rate: [0.20, 0.40]\n      fpr_at_operating_threshold: [0.25, 0.50]\n      hs_vs_text_acc_gap_32b: [0.00, 0.08]\n      cross_transfer_coverage_retention: [0.15, 0.35]\n    narrative_direction: \"Reframe as negative-result / diagnostic paper: 'Why Hidden-State Safety Monitoring Fails to Scale in Reasoning Models.' New story spine: (1) r1-8b shows temporal probes work in principle; (2) but the phenomenon is architecture/scale-specific — 32B models with different tokenizers/training distribute safety signal differently; (3) text-based monitoring is a surprisingly strong baseline at scale, suggesting safety commitment is textually explicit in larger models (less 'hidden'); (4) the Fisher-discriminant-in-minor-PCs finding explains WHY geometric methods fail universally even when the signal exists. Contribution becomes: principled negative result + diagnostic framework (Fisher analysis, layer sweep protocol, oracle ceiling methodology) that future work can build on. Target EMNLP Main as empirical analysis paper — negative results with strong methodology are publishable if the question matters and the analysis reveals why.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11771,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11771},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3db07e5b-d1ca-4a83-964c-45ed9f8b25b8","uuid":"55a0ade9-5d52-4e41-9326-27e64b518626"}
